SWT6621S DKMS package
====================

Install the matching headers for your kernel before installing this package.
On Armbian edge rockchip64, these are linux-headers-edge-rockchip64.
The package builds the SDIO transport, Wi-Fi and Bluetooth modules; it does
not package firmware, a device tree or prebuilt kernel modules.

Module loading, module parameters and board firmware paths are managed by
the distribution or administrator. This package installs no modules-load.d
or modprobe.d configuration. For firmware installed under
/lib/firmware/seekwave, the distribution should set firmware_dir=seekwave.

Packaging targets Debian 11+ and Ubuntu 20.04+. Kernel API compatibility is
separate: matching kernel headers and a supported driver/kernel combination
are required. The architecture-independent package contains source only.

Build on Debian/Ubuntu (older releases provide dh_dkms in dkms):

  sudo apt-get install git debhelper dkms build-essential
  # On releases with a separate dh-dkms package, install it as well.
  bash scripts/build-deb.sh

Commit or stash local changes first: the script packages the committed HEAD,
not uncommitted files. It exports that commit into a temporary directory and
leaves the checkout unchanged. Packages are written to dist/ by default; an
optional argument selects another output directory.

Install:

  sudo apt install ./dist/swt6621s-dkms_*_all.deb
  dkms status

Kernel image and header package hooks invoke DKMS automatically. Reinstalling
the same kernel normally reuses an existing DKMS build; a fresh kernel
version requires a new build. Do not remove a running kernel to test this.

The package does not forcibly unload a running driver during installation.
Reboot or reload the three modules to use the newly built modules.

Snapshot versions use the checked-out commit's UTC committer timestamp and
the first 12 characters of its ID: YYYY.MM.DD.HHMMSS+gitCOMMIT. Using the
commit time rather than the build time keeps the version stable across
rebuilds. Seconds distinguish successive commits on the same day; the hash
identifies the exact source. Backdated commits, or commits with identical
timestamps, do not guarantee increasing Debian version ordering.

The version is generated after the commit exists; it never needs to be
embedded into that same commit. dh_dkms substitutes #MODULE_VERSION# in
dkms.conf with the package version. The generated /usr/src/swt6621s-VERSION
directory and DKMS registration therefore use the same version as the deb.
The unprocessed dkms.conf is a packaging template; build the deb before
installing with DKMS. Direct dpkg-buildpackage builds remain available and
use the release version recorded in debian/changelog.

GitHub Actions runs the same script on pushes, pull requests and manual
dispatches. Push/manual builds use the selected source commit; pull request
builds explicitly check out the PR head rather than the temporary merge
commit. Download the deb and checksums from the workflow's artifact. The
workflow builds an architecture-independent source package on Debian 12;
it does not compile the modules for each supported kernel or test hardware.

Tags and package versions use exactly the same name, without a v prefix.
After committing and testing the source, create and push a tag manually:

  version=$(bash scripts/version.sh)
  git tag -a "$version" -m "Release $version"
  git push origin "refs/tags/$version"

To select another tested commit, pass it to scripts/version.sh and use that
same commit as the final argument to git tag. Annotated and lightweight
tags both resolve to the source commit for version generation.

CI never creates or pushes tags. Ordinary branch pushes, pull requests and
manual builds only upload Actions artifacts. Pushing a tag whose name
matches the source version additionally creates a draft GitHub Release
with the deb and SHA256SUMS attached. Review it and publish it manually.
The release command uses --verify-tag, so a missing tag cannot be created
implicitly. Other tag names do not produce a release; numeric tag names
that do not match the source version fail validation.
